        "line": 796,
        "label": "Exercise 12.B1 — Medium: Return to physical sensor errors",
        "level": "Medium",
        "source_sha256": "c246dca4323f90c9d390c74dbd253872564627c225e24321b363fb2e4ffba5dc",
        "source_text": "\nExercise 12.B1 — Medium: Return to physical sensor errors\nSuppose $x_1=(T-40)/10$ and $x_2=(d-0.5)/0.1$, with $T$ in degrees Celsius and distance $d$ in metres. Hardware errors satisfy $|\\Delta T|\\le0.2$ degrees and $|\\Delta d|\\le0.002$ metres. Find the correction-error bound. Does a tighter allowance of 0.06 degrees pass?\nReview: Sensitivity and certified radii.\nShow hintTransform the sensor-error box into normalized coordinates and bound its Euclidean radius.\nShow worked solutionBoth normalized coordinate errors are at most 0.02, so the radius is $\\sqrt{2}(0.02)\\approx0.028284$. This SDP certificate bounds physical correction error by $2\\sqrt{4/3}\\sqrt2(0.02)\\approx0.065320$ degrees, so it cannot approve the tighter 0.06-degree allowance. That does not establish a physical violation. In fact, the physical correction's regional gradients are $(0,0),(1,2),(1,-2),(2,0)$. Their largest $\\ell_1$ norm is 3, giving exact box error at most $3(0.02)=0.06$ degrees, attained within a one-active region. Thus an exact box analysis passes the non-strict allowance. Scaling both hardware-error bounds by at most $0.06/(2\\sqrt{4/3}\\sqrt2(0.02))\\approx0.918559$ would instead make the original SDP certificate sufficient. Treating the numerical metre and temperature errors as coordinates in one unscaled Euclidean norm would misrepresent the preprocessing.\n",
        "text_sha256": "5596806dde3c32e8d9487581c2668b6365e7623d008b47c7bce99a5d46fb214a"
      },
      "discharged": true,
      "remaining_missing_clauses": [],
      "completion_correspondence": "Sensor proves the actual positive SDP bound has square8/1875, encloses its six-decimal rounding.065320 and the normalized radius.028284 by strict half-unit bounds, and proves the SDP bound>.06. Genuine scaled raw hardware inequalities imply error<=s*sdpBound for s>=0. The precise threshold is.06 divided by the exact positive SDP bound, equals3sqrt6/8, is sufficient iff s is no larger, and rounds to.918559. The rounded denominator.065320 instead gives.918555 and is explicitly distinguished. The original Source59 conditional full literal is byte-exactly the actual Source60 B1 record, so its corrected exact denominator is now the current literal; the old peer body/time is retained.",
